## The idea

In a tournament tree every parent holds the smaller of its two children, so the root holds the overall winner (the minimum). The second smallest must have **lost directly to the winner** at some point — anything else lost to someone who is not the minimum, so at least two values are smaller than it. So walk down the winner's path from the root and look only at the opponents it beat; the smallest of those is the answer.

## Step by step

1. A single leaf has no second smallest → throw.
2. Start at the root. At each node, the child with the same value as the node is the winner's path; the other child is the opponent it beat.
3. Keep the smallest opponent value seen.
4. Move down into the winner's child and repeat until reaching a leaf.

## Worked example

**Input:** root 2 → (2 → leaves 2, 5), (7 → leaves 7, 9)

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| root (2) | left child is 2 (winner path), right child 7 is the opponent → best = 7 |
| node 2 | left leaf 2 is the winner, right leaf 5 is the opponent → best = min(7, 5) = 5 |
| leaf 2 | reached the winner's leaf → stop |

**Result:** 5

## Cost

O(height) time — we follow one path only, O(log n) for a balanced tournament. O(1) extra memory.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// TreeNode is provided (Models.cs): Value, Left, Right. Each parent holds the minimum of its children.
public static class TournamentTree {

    public static int SecondSmallest(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(root));
        if (root.Left == null && root.Right == null)
            throw new InvalidOperationException("A single leaf has no second smallest.");

        int best = int.MaxValue;
        TreeNode current = root;

        // Follow the winner down. Every opponent it beat is a candidate for second place.
        while (current.Left != null || current.Right != null) {
            TreeNode winner;
            TreeNode opponent;
            if (current.Left != null && current.Left.Value == current.Value) {
                winner = current.Left;
                opponent = current.Right;
            } else {
                winner = current.Right;
                opponent = current.Left;
            }
            if (opponent != null && opponent.Value < best) best = opponent.Value;
            current = winner;
        }
        return best;
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: A single leaf? Duplicate leaves?**

No second smallest → throw; the statement says unique

**Q: Why O(height)?**

Only the opponents of the minimum can be second

**Q: Shared state?**

Read-only tree

**Q: Find the k-th smallest.**

Replay matches along the winner's path, k times — or a heap of opponents

**Q: How would you test it?**

Build random tournaments and compare with sorted leaves

